### Ran Playwright code
```js
await page.evaluate('() => (({scenery: window.KBG.scenery.length, sprites: window.KBG.scenery.reduce((m,p)=>(m[p.sprite]=(m[p.sprite]||0)+1,m),{}), sizes: Object.fromEntries(Object.entries(window.KBG.sprites||{}).slice(0,3)), artSizes: (()=>{ const out={}; for (const k of [\'oak1\',\'oak2\',\'pine1\',\'pine2\',\'poplar\',\'bush\',\'rock\',\'flowers\',\'fence\',\'boat\']) { const im=document.querySelector(\'canvas\'); } return out; })()}))');
```

140x180. Not huge. Yet 6ms per drawImage under SwiftShader maybe because SVG image rasterizes every draw? Once decoded, should be texture. With software renderer, scaling image with alpha and transform maybe slow but 73*? Possibly each drawImage of an SVG image triggers re-rasterization at target size because SVG images are vector and not cached as bitmap? Chrome caches decoded SVG but maybe at intrinsic size; drawing scaled triggers raster each time at scale. 73 draws with different scales (~1) but sway transform each frame changes the transform causing re-raster each frame. Vector rasterization of complex paths could be expensive! Yes, SVG images are rasterized on GPU each draw or cached per scale. Animated sway transform changes sampling but shouldn't re-raster source.

Buildings drawn (18) are cheap? They are also SVG but larger; we found buildings off gave 495ms vs 415 with both — noise, actually disabling buildings seemed slower due to variance. So scenery and buildings together ~400ms.

Given Chrome's SVG rasterization in software, 73 trees x 6ms. Could pre-rasterize all SVG images to bitmaps once (create canvas at e.g. 2x natural size) and draw from canvas. That should drastically reduce cost because PNG raster already. Do this in loadSprite after image load: draw to offscreen canvas at natural size (or 1.5x) and store canvas in images. But canvas drawImage of SVG onto 2D canvas also rasterizes once. Then subsequent draws use raster canvas. Let's test: easiest modify loadSprite to create a canvas copy. But `img.height/img.width` usage works with canvas too (HTMLCanvasElement has width/height). Art aspect fine. For building quality at zoom max 1.7 and scale .5, artW ~100-250; source 140x180 is enough; buildings may have intrinsic 260x300. Could render at 2x for retina/zoom, then draw. Memory fine.

