Light costs ~116ms; grain ~220ms. Both full-screen compositing. Need fix both. Options:

1. Bake light vignette into bgCanvas (static water background) — but the map covers the center, and light should darken map edges too. Could draw vignette into the map cache after scene (on top of map) and bg cache; that bakes it into static layers. Dynamic effects then drawn on top of vignette would not be darkened, but effects are small; acceptable. However light gradient also darkens top/bottom of screen. If we bake into both bg and map canvases, no CSS overlay needed. But when the map cache rebuilds, vignette redrawn. Great. Remove .world-light or keep it display:none.

2. Grain: bake a subtle noise texture into static layers too (bg+map), or drop it altogether. Could generate a small noise canvas once and tile over map cache with low alpha at build time. But the map layer is redrawn on pan; noise would be stationary in world view (will shift with pan rebuild) which is fine. Bake noise into both bg and map static caches. For dynamic effects no grain, okay.

Alternatively use CSS `background-image` noise with normal opacity (no blend) — likely much cheaper than mix-blend-mode; but still a full-screen layer composited above animated canvas each frame. Normal alpha compositing of a static layer might still cost, but not 220ms. Still, baking is best.

Implementation:
- Remove `.world-light` and `.world-grain` elements? They may be used for aesthetic. Keep element but set no blend and maybe remove from DOM in render? Better bake into caches and hide them via CSS display:none or delete elements. Delete from index.html and CSS rules accordingly? Clean approach: remove divs and CSS blocks.

Baking:
In rebuildCaches after drawing bg base: draw a `drawVignette()` using same gradients onto bgCtx. Also add grain overlay if desired.

In map cache after scene: draw vignette again so map edges darken. But light overlay composited over both bg and map separately with alpha means the result is equivalent-ish: original overlay on top of everything. Drawing vignette into bg and map separately yields overlay applied to each layer, then map alpha over bg; close enough. The map is transparent outside island, so bg vignette applies.

Also light has top/bottom gradient: apply to both.

Grain: create `grainTile` canvas via generating noise pixels 128x128 (ImageData random alpha). Then pattern fill over bg and map at low alpha. But if each layer gets grain, the map area gets double grain (bg grain beneath transparent map contributes, plus map grain). Fine, slight. Or only apply grain on bg and map? Better create single `drawScreenFinish()` that draws vignette and grain; in map cache, apply vignette+grain; in bg cache, apply too. We could apply grain to bg only, but it would be covered by opaque map layer where the island is. The map only covers tiles/road/building; the water area is transparent (bg shows). For grain over land, need map grain. Do both.

We can also keep grain subtle with alpha .05 normal blending.

